  ],
  "resolved_items": [
    {
      "item": "judge classifications differ",
      "resolution": "Resolve as faithful-equivalent for the selected equation-form assertion. The source introduces a differential equation; the target characterizes its classical pointwise satisfaction. Neither asserts that arbitrary fields satisfy the equation.",
      "primary_evidence": "The supplied primary-source rendering, raw PDF page 23, printed page 1, introduces (1.1) with the phrase 'has the form'. The elaborated target is an equivalence, and D001 unfolds through D002 to the same derivative-and-equation condition displayed on its right."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge requested adjudication",
      "resolution": "The requested scope and solution-concept review identifies no missing source conclusion. A definition of the displayed equation need not assert global satisfaction or characterize every subsequently possible generalized solution concept.",
      "primary_evidence": "The paragraph surrounding (1.1) specifies the equation, domains, coefficients, and partial-derivative notation. It states no existence theorem or generalized solution definition. D002 defines satisfaction at the supplied point."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge classification is undetermined",
      "resolution": "The identified ambiguities do not prevent acceptance of this equation definition. Classical derivative existence explicates the displayed notation, and the zero-dimensional extension preserves all source cases. Neither constitutes a substantive strengthening.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 1 identifies subscripts as partial derivatives and expressly includes m = 1 in the following paragraph. The target includes every positive finite dimension and imposes no external regularity hypothesis on q."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C02 unclear",
      "resolution": "C02 passes. Curried real arguments and Fin m coordinates represent the stated domain and codomain. The matching matrix indices preserve dimensions. Allowing m = 0 supplies a harmless empty-coordinate extension of the definition, without excluding any described system.",
      "primary_evidence": "The source specifies q : R × R → R^m and a constant real m × m matrix. D013, D014, D020, and D026 give finite coordinate indexing, complete enumeration, real matrix entries, and natural-number dimension."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C03 unclear",
      "resolution": "C03 passes. Universal quantification makes the characterization available at every point; it does not assert the predicate everywhere. This preserves the source's equation-form status. For a fixed q and A, universally quantifying either side of the characterization also gives equivalent global classical satisfaction conditions.",
      "primary_evidence": "The target has scope ∀ m q A x t, P(q,A,x,t) ↔ ∃ qt qx, derivative conditions ∧ residual equality. The source introduces an equation for unknown functions, without asserting satisfaction by a particular field."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C04 unclear",
      "resolution": "C04 passes for the selected classical display. Existence of the two partial derivatives is a defined satisfaction condition, not an added hypothesis restricting the theorem's applicability. No joint differentiability, continuous partial derivatives, or global smoothness class is required.",
      "primary_evidence": "The source explicitly interprets subscripts as partial derivatives. D002 uses HasDerivAt on τ ↦ q x τ and ξ ↦ q ξ t. D016 uses the full neighborhood filter at the differentiation point. D003 supplies continuity of scalar multiplication, not continuity of q."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C10 unclear",
      "resolution": "C10 passes. The finite real setting and scalar specialization are preserved. Empty dimension is a conservative definitional extension. The target does not claim to characterize weak solutions, and the selected paragraph supplies no such characterization to preserve.",
      "primary_evidence": "The source gives real finite-dimensional objects and then m = 1. D001 specializes D002 to Fin m; D043, D047, and D074 provide the usual coordinate vector structure and product topology."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C11 unclear",
      "resolution": "C11 passes. Actual derivative witnesses prevent satisfaction through default values at nondifferentiable points. Two-sided derivatives match the unrestricted real domains. At m = 0 the condition is consistently trivial, while positive-dimensional satisfaction remains nontrivial.",
      "primary_evidence": "D002 requires both HasDerivAt assertions. D046 supplies coordinatewise zero, and D021 supplies rowwise matrix action. For m = 1, constant fields satisfy the condition, whereas q(x,t) = t has time derivative 1 and spatial derivative 0 and fails it for every coefficient."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item N06 unclear",
      "resolution": "N06 passes. Existential derivative values are not an assertion that a solution field exists. The target adds no rank, invertibility, spectral, or hyperbolicity requirement and makes no well-posedness claim.",
      "primary_evidence": "The source separately says further properties of A are required for hyperbolicity. The target universally binds q and A before the equivalence and existentially binds only qt and qx inside its satisfaction condition."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left implication translation_implies_source unclear",
      "resolution": "The implication holds at the selected equation-definition level. The reconstructed condition gives exactly the time partial derivative plus the fixed matrix acting on the spatial partial derivative, equal to vector zero, at arbitrary real arguments. No global solution assertion is needed to recover the introduced equation form.",
      "primary_evidence": "Equation (1.1) and its derivative convention match D002 and D016. D021 uses each matrix row dotted with qx; D045 and D046 give componentwise addition and zero."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left implication source_implies_translation unclear",
      "resolution": "The implication holds under the source's ordinary partial-derivative convention and the supplied definition. Existing partial derivatives supply qt and qx, and the displayed equation supplies the residual equality. Conversely those witnesses express the same classical condition. The empty-dimensional characterization follows from the same definition without a new substantive source assertion.",
      "primary_evidence": "The source supplies the real domains, matrix dimensions, equation, and derivative roles. D001 and D002 identify the left predicate with precisely these derivative witnesses and equality, using the explicit real product-space instances."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "lean_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "yes",
      "reasoning": "As an equation definition, the target reproduces (1.1) under its stated real-domain and partial-derivative conventions. Unfolding D001 and D002 yields actual time and space derivative vectors satisfying qt + A qx = 0, with ordinary left matrix action and vector zero. The characterization applies at every point without asserting arbitrary or global solution satisfaction."
    },
    "source_implies_lean": {
      "verdict": "yes",
      "reasoning": "The displayed equation, read classically where asserted, requires precisely the two partial derivatives and their zero residual. Representing their values by existential witnesses and currying the domain preserves that condition. The defined predicate therefore admits the stated equivalence. No additional smoothness premise or excluded source case is introduced; the empty-dimensional instance is a harmless definitional extension."
    }
  },
  "classification": "faithful-equivalent",
  "accepted": true,
  "remaining_uncertainties": [
    "The selected paragraph does not specify a broader generalized solution concept or a global regularity class. Acceptance concerns the classical meaning of its displayed equation and does not establish equivalence with weak or distributional satisfaction.",
    "The source does not explicitly discuss zero-component systems. The formal empty-dimensional instance is accepted as a conservative definitional convention, not as an asserted physical case or substantive strengthening.",
    "Primary-source inspection used the supplied page rendering. The PDF bytes and supplied provenance hashes were not independently recomputed."
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "definition-versus-solution-assertion",
      "evidence": "The source introduces the system's equation form. D001 and D002 make the audited equivalence definitionally reflexive, while its satisfaction predicate distinguishes constant fields from the scalar field q(x,t) = t.",
      "impact": "Definitional reflexivity is appropriate here and does not make solution satisfaction vacuous. The target must not be reported as a solution-existence or global-satisfaction theorem."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "regularity-and-applicability",
      "evidence": "HasDerivAt occurs within the predicate on both sides of an equivalence universally quantified over all q. There is no C1, joint differentiability, or spectral premise.",
      "impact": "The target does not reduce theorem applicability through additional hypotheses. Its explicit classical derivative semantics are not grounds for a faithful-stronger classification."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The disagreement turns on whether unspecified extensions of the source's equation notation must themselves be formalized. The primary page selects an equation-form introduction, not a comprehensive solution theory. The supplied declarations preserve its objects, derivative roles, coefficient constancy, matrix orientation, and exact residual. Rechecking the disputed dependencies confirms ordinary real coordinate topology and genuine slice derivatives, with no hidden regularity restriction on q. Pointwise characterization preserves the equation's meaning and can be universally closed for global classical satisfaction without confusing that closure with an assertion that every field solves it. Both semantic implication directions hold at this definitional level. The empty-dimensional convention excludes no source case, and positive-dimensional examples establish nonvacuity. Acceptance follows from those primary semantic correspondences, not from agreement counts or provenance hashes."
}
